-> Private IP: Used for communication within the VPC. It is automatically assigned and is not accessible from the Internet.
-> Public IP: Used for Internet access. It is automatically assigned (if enabled) and may change when the instance is stopped and started.
-> Elastic IP: A static public IPv4 address that remains the same until you release it. It is commonly used for production servers that require a permanent public IP.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Action             |  Private IP |         Public IP          |                Elastic IP                                 |
| ------------------ | ----------- | -------------------------- |-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Start Instance     | ✅ Same     | ❌ May change             | ✅ Same                                                   |
| Stop Instance      | ✅ Retained | ❌ Released               | ✅ Same (if associated)                                   |
| Start After Stop   | ✅ Same     | ❌ New Public IP assigned | ✅ Same                                                   |
| Reboot Instance    | ✅ Same     | ✅ Same                   | ✅ Same                                                   |
| Terminate Instance | ❌ Lost     | ❌ Lost                   | ✅ Returned to your AWS account (if it was an Elastic IP) |
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Question
--------------------------------
Q: Can we delete an Elastic IP?
Answer:
Yes. An Elastic IP can be released (deleted) from your AWS account. If it is associated with an EC2 instance, you must first disassociate it and then release it. Once released, the IP address is returned to AWS and cannot be guaranteed to be allocated to you again.

