-> What is VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)?
--------------------------------------
A VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) is a virtual network within the AWS cloud
that provides a secure, isolated environment for you to run your AWS
resources. It free Service in AWS. It is region specific.


-> Important Terminologies
---------------------------
Types of IPs: IPv4 vs IPv6

IPV4 (Internet Protocol Version 4)
------------------------------------
IPv4 is the most widely used version of the Internet Protocol. It uses
32-bit addresses, which are divided into four octets (each 8 bits long).

The format of an IPv4 address is a series of four decimal numbers
separated by periods, with each number ranging from 0 to 255. This
results in a total of 4.3 billion unique IP addresses.

Example: 192.168.32.101

IPv4 addresses are running out due to the explosion in the number of
internet-connected devices. This is why we are seeing a transition to
IPv6.



IPV6 (Internet Protocol Version 6)
----------------------------------
IPv6 was introduced to address the limitations of IPv4, particularly the
exhaustion of IP addresses. It uses 128-bit addresses, which are split
into eight octets.

The format of an IPv6 address is alphanumeric and uses colons to
separate each block of the address. It provides a far larger address
space compared to IPv4.

Example: 2001:0db8 : 85a3 :0000 : 0000 : 8a2e : 0370:7334

IPv6 can accommodate a virtually unlimited number of unique IP addresses
(around 340 undecillion addresses, which is a number so large it could
assign unique addresses to every atom on Earth many times over). This
allows for growth in the number of devices connected to the internet
without worrying about running out of addresses.

-> CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing)-Sider
---------------------------------------
CIDR is used to define IP address ranges and to allocate IP addresses
efficiently.

CIDR notation represents IP addresses with a suffix that indicates the
number of bits used for the network (the subnet portion) of the address

Example: 10.0.0.0/24

	 172.31.0.0/16--------->2^(32-16)=65536
	 172.31.0.0/24--------->2^(32-24)=




VPC--->CIDR---->Reserved IP Addresses 172.31.0.0/16
_________________________________________________________
|							|
|     172.31.16.0/20   172.31.32.0/20	172.31.0.0/20	|
|	---------	 ---------	 ---------	|
|	|	|	 |	 |	 |     	 |	|
|	|	|	 |	 |	 |	 |	|			|
|	---------	 ---------	 ---------	|
|	Subnet-1	  Subnet-2	  Subnet-3	|
|_______________________________________________________|



A subnet is a segment of the IP address range of your VPC that allows
you to organize and isolate your resources based on their function,
access needs, or security requirements.

-> Types of Subnets:
-----------------
Public Subnet:
--------------
A subnet that is connected to the internet. Resources in
this subnet (like web servers) can send and receive traffic from the
internet.

Incoming and Outgoing Traffic: Public subnets allow both inbound and
outbound traffic. For example, if you launch an EC2 instance in a public
subnet, it can be accessed directly from the internet.

Private Subnet:
----------------
A subnet that does not have direct internet access.
Resources in private subnets (like database servers) cannot directly
access or be accessed from the internet.



-> Route Table
----------------
A route table defines how traffic should be routed within the VPC and
to/from external networks.


-> Internet Gateway (IGW)
---------------------------
An Internet Gateway (IGW) is a component that allows communication
between instances in your VPC and the internet.

Note: If an Internet Gateway is associated with a route table for a
      subnet, that subnet is considered a public subnet.

Attach an Internet Gateway (IGW) to your VPC: This will allow internet
traffic to flow into and out of your VPC.


-> NAT Gateway
-----------
Definition: A NAT Gateway (Network Address Translation Gateway) is a
managed AWS service that allows instances in a private subnet to access
the internet, but it does not allow inbound traffic from the internet to
those instances. Essentially, a NAT Gateway provides only outbound
internet access for resources in a private subnet.

Use Case: Resources in a private subnet, like a database or application
server, may need to access the internet for tasks such as downloading
updates

How It Works: When an instance in a private subnet tries to access the
internet, the traffic is routed to the NAT Gateway, which then forwards
the request to the internet and sends the response back to the instance.
This keeps the instance in the private subnet protected from direct
inbound internet traffic.

Cost: The NAT Gateway is a commercial service in AWS, meaning you are
charged for its use. There are costs associated with data processing
(how much data goes through the NAT Gateway) and data transfer (outbound
traffic to the internet). The pricing can accumulate quickly, so it's
important to monitor usage carefully.


