Example block to create ec2-instance

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
	ami= "ami-12345678"
	instance_type= "t2.micro"
	key_name= "my-key-pair"
	subnet_id= "subnet-abc123"

	security_groups = ["sg-123456"]

	tags = {
	   Name = "MyEC2Instance"
	   Environment = "Production"
	}

	monitoring = true
	disable_api_termination = false
}


# resource "aws_instance" "example"
--------------------------------
resource: This is a block in Terraform
define a specific piece of infrastructure (a resource).
In this case, it's defining an AWS EC2 instance.

"aws_instance": This specifies the type of resource you
are creating. Here, it's an EC2 instance from Amazon Web
Services (AWS).

"example": This is the name of the resource. It serves
as an identifier for the instance in the Terraform
configuration. You will refer to this name in other
parts of the code or for outputs.

Inside the resource block:
ami = "ami-12345678":

ami: This attribute specifies the Amazon Machine Image
(AMI) that will be used to launch the EC2 instance.
Value: "ami-12345678" is a placeholder AMI ID. You would
replace this with a real AMI ID that corresponds to the
OS or application image you want to use (e.g., an Ubuntu
or Amazon Linux AMI).
instance_type = "t2.micro":

instance_type: Specifies the instance type for the EC2.
Instance types determine the CPU, memory, and networking
capacity for the EC2 instance.

key_name: This specifies the name of the SSH key pair
that will be used to access the EC2 instance securely.
Value: "my-key-pair" is the name of the SSH key pair
that exists in your AWS account. You need to have this
key pair created ahead of time in the AWS console, or
Terraform will create one if specified.
subnet_id = "subnet-abc123":

subnet_id: This attribute specifies the subnet in which
the EC2 instance will be launched.
Value: "subnet-abc123" is a placeholder value for the
subnet ID. You need to replace it with the actual ID of
a subnet in your VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) where you
want the instance to reside.
security_groups = ["sg-123456"]:


security_groups: Specifies one or more security groups
to associate with the EC2 instance. Security groups
define the firewall rules for the instance, controlling
inbound and outbound traffic.
Value: The value is a list of security group IDs. In
this case, the instance will be associated with the
security group "sg-123456". You can add multiple
security group IDs if needed.
tags = { ... }:


tags: Tags are key-value pairs that you can assign to
resources for identification and management purposes.
Value: The value is a map containing tags for the
instance.
Name = "MyEC2Instance": This tag sets the name of the
instance to "MyEC2Instance".
Environment = "Production": This tag assigns the
environment type as "Production".
These tags help organize and manage resources in AWS,
especially when you have a large number of them.
monitoring = true:


monitoring: This attribute enables or disables
CloudWatch detailed monitoring for the EC2 instance.
Value: true enables detailed monitoring, which provides
additional metrics (such as CPU utilization, disk I/0,
etc.) every minute. This is typically used for
performance tracking and alerting.
disable_api_termination = false:


disable_api_termination: This setting controls whether
the instance can be terminated via the AWS API (for
example, when running aws ec2 terminate-instances).
Value: false means that the instance can be terminated
via the API. If set to true, it would prevent the
instance from being accidentally terminated.

Summary:
This Terraform configuration block defines an EC2
instance on AWS with the following settings:

AMI ID ami-12345678 (an Amazon Machine Image).
Instance type of t2.micro, suitable for low-cost or
small applications.
Uses an existing SSH key pair called my-key-pair.
Launched in a specific subnet identified by subnet-
abc123.
Security group sg-123456 is assigned to control network
access.
Two tags: one for Name and one for Environment, to help
identify and organize the instance.
CloudWatch monitoring is enabled.
API termination is allowed (i.e., the instance can be
terminated via the API).


